25: \"¿Qué quieres decir con eso?\" (What do you mean by that?) Useful.\n\n- Cue 126: \"Que me invites a la próxima cena.\" (That you invite me to the next dinner.) Useful.\n\n- Cue 127: \"Trato hecho.\" (Deal.) Useful.\n\n- Cue 128: \"Aquí tienen. ¡Que aproveche!\" (Here you go. Enjoy your meal!) Useful.\n\n- Cue 129: \"¡Qué buena pinta!\" (Looks delicious!) Useful.\n\n- Cue 130: \"Por el piso nuevo.\" (For the new flat.) Useful.\n\n- Cue 131: \"¡Salud!\" (Cheers!) Useful.\n\n- Cue 132: \"Por cierto, ¿sigue lloviendo?\" (By the way, is it still raining?) Useful.\n\n- Cue 133: \"A cántaros.\" (Raining cats and dogs.) Useful.\n\n- Cue 134: \"Entonces me vuelves a prestar el paraguas.\" (Then you will lend me the umbrella again.) Useful.\n\n- Cue 135: \"Ni hablar. Esta vez te acompaño a casa.\" (No way. This time I will walk you home.) Useful.\n\n- Cue 136: \"Eres un sol.\" (You are a sunshine.) Useful.\n\nAll of these are quite useful. For the lesson we need 20 phrases; choose 4 per part.\n\nWe should aim to choose a spread across variety: greetings, ordering, directions, interactions, health, house, etc.\n\nPart1: cues 1-30. Need 4.\n\nPossible picks:\n\n- 1: \"¡Buenos días! ¿Qué te pongo?\" (Used in cafes)\n- 2: \"Un café con leche, por favor.\" (Ordering)\n- 5: \"Claro. ¿Algo más?\" (Yes, anything else)\n- 9: \"Sí, sin problema.\" (Yes, no problem) maybe too generic; could choose 8: \"¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta?\" (Can I pay by card?)\n\nOr we could choose the phrase \"Para llevar. Tengo un poco de prisa.\" (To go, I'm a bit in a hurry.)\n\nLet's pick 4 distinct topics:\n\n- Phrase 1: \"¡Buenos días! ¿Qué te pongo?\"\n- Phrase 2: \"Un café con leche, por favor.\"\n- Phrase 8: \"¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta?\"\n- Phrase 10 (modified): \"¿Qué haces por aquí?\" after removing name \"Lena\". Actually original \"¡Lena! ¿Qué haces por aquí?\" but we might remove name: \"¿Qué haces por aquí?\" (what are you doing here?) Could be a phrase.\n\nAlternatively choose phrase 20: \"Oye, va a llover. ¿Tienes paraguas?\" But that's part2 maybe. Let's keep for part1 the phrase \"¿Para llevar? Tengo un poco de prisa.\" Could be phrase 4.\n\nThus part1 picks (maybe 4):\n\n1. 1: \"¡Buenos días! ¿Qué te pongo?\" (greeting and question)\n\n2. 2: \"Un café con leche, por favor.\"\n\n3. 8: \"¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta?\"\n\n4. 10 (modified): \"¿Qué haces por aquí?\" Might be a phrase for conversation.\n\nAlternatively \"¿Algo más?\" is simpler but we can include.\n\nBetter include phrase about hurry: \"Tengo un poco de prisa.\" but that is part of phrase \"Para llevar. Tengo un poco de prisa.\" Two sentences. Only one phrase per cue; maybe we pick the second sentence as phrase: \"Tengo un poco de prisa.\" It's a phrase of 5 words, good.\n\nThus we can choose:\n\n- Cue 4: \"Para llevar. Tengo un poco de prisa.\" Actually it's two sentences; we could pick \"Tengo un poco de prisa.\"\n\nWe must keep phrase textual exactly as in the subtitle line. The cue text contains \"Para llevar. Tengo un poco de prisa.\" Since phrase is entire cue, we might keep both sentences? The rule: phrase should be up to 12 words. That's fine: we can keep the whole cue as phrase: \"Para llevar. Tengo un poco de prisa.\" But maybe better to split. But we can keep as is.\n\nThus from part1 we can have 4 phrases: #1, #2, #8, #4.\n\nLet's mark them.\n\nPart2: cues 31-53 (00:02:05 to 00:03:36). Choose 4 phrases.\n\nPotential choices:\n\n- 31: \"Perdone, ¿me puede ayudar?\" (Excuse me, could you help me?)\n\n- 33: \"Busco el tren para el centro.\" (I'm looking for the train to the city centre.)\n\n- 36: \"A las diez y cuarto, pero hoy va con retraso.\" (At quarter past ten, but it's delayed.)\n\n- 38: \"Unos veinte minutos, más o menos.\" (About twenty minutes, more or less.)\n\nAlternatively include \"¿Puede repetirlo, por favor? Más despacio.\" (Could you repeat that, please? Slower.)\n\nAlternatively include \"¿Cuántas habitaciones tiene?\" (How many bedrooms does it have?) but that's later part.\n\nBut for part2 we can pick:\n\n- 31: phrase #31\n\n- 33: phrase #33\n\n- 36: phrase #36\n\n- 38: phrase #38\n\nWe could also choose #35: \"¿A qué hora sale el próximo?\" but limited 4.\n\nThus part2 picks #31, #33, #36, #38.\n\nPart3: cues 54-80 (covers 00:03:47 to 00:05:35). Choose 4.\n\nPotential picks:\n\n- 54: \"Pasa, pasa. Este es el salón.\" - Maybe redundant, but okay.\n\n- 55: \"¡Qué luminoso!\" (How bright!) Could be.\n\n- 56: \"Da al sur, así que tiene sol toda la tarde.\" (It faces south.)\n\n- 57: \"¿Cuántas habitaciones tiene?\" (How many bedrooms does it have?)\n\n- 58: \"Dos habitaciones y un baño.\"\n\n- 59: \"¿Puedo ver la cocina?\" (Can I see the kitchen?)\n\n- 60: \"Por supuesto. Está recién reformada.\"\n\n- 61: \"¿Cuánto es el alquiler al mes?\"\n\n- 62: \"Ochocientos euros, gastos incluidos.\"\n\n- 63: \"¿Incluye la calefacción?\" (Does it include heating?)\n\n- 64: \"Sí, la calefacción y el agua.\"\n\n- 65: \"¿Hay que pagar fianza?\" (Do we have to pay a deposit?)\n\n- 66: \"Dos meses de fianza.\"\n\n- 67: \"¿Se admiten mascotas?\" (Are pets allowed?)\n\n- 68: \"Depende. ¿Qué mascota tienes?\" (It depends. What pet do you have?)\n\n- 71: \"¿Cuándo estaría disponible?\" (When would it be available?)\n\n- 73: \"Hay otra persona interesada, la verdad.\"\n\n- 74: \"Entiendo. Déjeme pensarlo.\"\n\n- 78: \"Menos mal que llevo paraguas.\"\n\n- 79: \"Buenas tardes. ¿En qué le puedo ayudar?\" Might be good.\n\n- 80: \"No me encuentro muy bien.\" (I'm not feeling well.)\n\nThus pick 4 from part3 covering apartment, rent, health.\n\nPick:\n\n- 57: \"¿Cuántas habitaciones tiene?\" (Apartment question)\n\n- 61: \"¿Cuánto es el alquiler al mes?\" (Rent question)\n\n- 66: \"Dos meses de fianza.\" (Deposit)\n\n- 78: \"Menos mal que llevo paraguas.\" (Lucky I have umbrella). Or maybe pick 80: \"No me encuentro muy bien.\" (I'm not feeling well.)\n\nWe want some variety, maybe 60 (Por supuesto. Está recién reformada.) but choose 4.\n\nPick #57, #61, #66, #78.\n\nHowever #78 is about umbrella, but that appears later part (as part3). That phrase is useful. Let's go for #57 (rooms), #61 (rent), #66 (deposit), #78 (umbrella). That's 4.\n\nPart4: cues 81-108 (00:05:38-00:07:27). Choose 4.\n\nPotential picks:\n\n- 81: \"¿Qué le pasa?\" (What's wrong?)\n\n- 82: \"Me duele la garganta y estoy muy cansada.\" (My throat hurts and I'm very tired.)\n\n- 87: \"Le recomiendo estas pastillas para la garganta.\" (I recommend these throat lozenges.)\n\n- 90: \"Mejor después de comer.\" (Better after eating.)\n\n- 92: \"¿Y si no mejoro?\" (And if I don't improve?)\n\n- 94: \"De acuerdo. ¿Cuánto le debo?\" (Alright. How much do I owe you?)\n\n- 101: \"No te preocupes. Acabo de llegar.\"\n\n- 103: \"¿Qué tal la visita?\" (How was the visit?)\n\n- 107: \"¿Vas a quedártelo?\" (Are you going to keep it?)\n\n- 108: \"Creo que sí. Mañana le escribo a la dueña.\" (I think so.)\n\n- 115: \"Enseguida.\" (Right away.)\n\n- 116: \"Oye, ¿estás bien? Tienes mala cara.\" (Hey, are you ok? You look unwell.)\n\n- 117: \"Estoy un poco resfriada, nada más.\" (I'm a bit cold.)\n\n- 120: \"¿Te echo una mano con la mudanza?\" (Shall I give you a hand with the move?)\n\n- 124: \"Me debes dos. Pero ¿quién cuenta?\" (You owe me two, but who counts?)\n\n- 127: \"Trato hecho.\" (Deal.)\n\nThus pick 4 that are different: maybe #81, #87, #92, #120. Or #101 and #103. Let's decide.\n\n- #81 \"¿Qué le pasa?\" (What's wrong?)\n\n- #87 \"Le recomiendo estas pastillas para la garganta.\" (I recommend these throat lozenges.)\n\n- #92 \"¿Y si no mejoro?\" (And if I don't get better?)\n\n- #120 \"¿Te echo una mano con la mudanza?\" (Shall I give you a hand with the move?)\n\nThat's 4.\n\nPart5: cues 109-136 (00:07:32-00:09:18). Choose 4.\n\nPotential picks:\n\n- 109: \"¡Enhorabuena! Hay que celebrarlo.\" (Congratulations! We must celebrate it.)\n\n- 110: \"¿Qué van a tomar?\" (What are they going to drink?)\n\n- 112: \"La paella está muy buena hoy.\" (The paella is very good today.)\n\n- 115: \"Enseguida.\" (Right away.)\n\n- 116: \"Oye, ¿estás bien? Tienes mala cara.\" (Hey, are you okay? You look bad.)\n\n- 121: \"¿De verdad? Eso sería genial.\" (Really? That's great.)\n\n- 127: \"Trato hecho.\" (Deal.)\n\n- 130: \"Por el piso nuevo.\" (For the new flat.)\n\n- 131: \"¡Salud!\" (Cheers!)\n\n- 132: \"Por cierto, ¿sigue lloviendo?\" (By the way, is it still raining?)\n\n- 133: \"A cántaros.\" (Raining heavily.)\n\n- 135: \"Ni hablar. Esta vez te acompaño a casa.\" (No way. This time I will walk you home.)\n\n- 136: \"Eres un sol.\" (You're a sunshine); expression of appreciation.\n\nPick 4: maybe #112, #115, #127, #136 or #133. Let's pick a variety: #112 (paella), #115 (Enseguida), #130 (Por el piso nuevo) maybe about flats, #136 (Eres un sol). Or #133 (A cántaros) for idiomatic phrase.\n\nBetter incorporate phrases for the colon and idioms. Since it's intermediate level, \"Eres un sol\" is idiom meaning \"you are a sunshine\" maybe more advanced. Also \"A cántaros\" is idiom for \"raining heavily\". Choose those.\n\nThus picks for part5:\n\n- Cue 112: \"La paella está muy buena hoy.\"\n\n- Cue 133: \"A cántaros.\"\n\n- Cue 135: \"Ni hablar. Esta vez te acompaño a casa.\"\n\n- Cue 136: \"Eres un sol.\"\n\nAlternatively maybe need 4; we'll pick these.\n\nNow we have 20 phrases: list them and note their start times, context lines before/after, usage note.\n\nWe need to capture before and after lines: for each phrase we need the previous cue and next cue from Spanish subtitles. We need to copy them exactly as they appear (up to 15 words each). Let's list each selected phrase with its index and details.\n\nWe'll need to compute for each:\n\n- phrase number (assigned by order in sheet: after sorting in timestamp order). The sheet expects phrases in timestamp order.\n\nWe'll also need to compute which part each phrase belongs to, and compute \"Phrases per fifth\" numbers later. Since we will have exactly 4 per part, we can list in order: part1 (cues 1,2,4,8 maybe?), part2, part3, part4, part5.\n\nBut we need to replace with actual selected cues. Let's assign final selection order per timestamp.\n\nSelected cues:\n\nPart1: #1 (00:00:04), #2 (00:00:07), #4 (00:00:15), #8 (00:00:30). (We might also consider #4's phrase with two sentences; it's okay.\n\nPart2: #31 (00:02:05), #33 (00:02:12), #36 (00:02:23), #38 (00:02:32). It's okay.\n\nPart3: #57 (00:03:59), #61 (00:04:14), #66 (00:04:34), #78 (00:05:19). Wait #78 is actually part3? Actually #78 is at 00:05:19, which is in part3 (max is 00:05:35). Yes it's part3.\n\nPart4: #81 (00:05:38), #87 (00:06:00), #92 (00:06:20), #120 (00:08:15). Wait #120 is at start 00:08:15 which is part5 actually, not part4. Mist. For part4 we need selections within 00:05:38 to 00:07:28. So #120 is not in part4. My earlier choice for part4 includes #120, but that's part5. Let's adjust.\n\nWe need 4 phrases for part4. Let's pick others in part4: #81 \"¿Qué le pasa?\" (00:05:38), #87 \"Le recomiendo estas pastillas para la garganta.\" (00:06:00), #92 \"¿Y si no mejoro?\" (00:06:20), #103 \"¿Qué tal la visita?\" (00:07:09) or #107 \"¿Vas a quedártelo?\" (00:07:24). Let's pick #103 (00:07:09) to keep in part4.\n\nThus part4 picks: #81, #87, #92, #103.\n\nPart5 picks: #112 (00:07:43), #133 (00:09:03), #135 (00:09:11), #136 (00:09:16). That makes 4 in part5.\n\nNow we have total 20 phrases.\n\nNow we need to get context lines for each chosen phrase:\n\n- For each phrase, we need \"Before\" (the previous cue's text) and \"After\" (the next cue's text). We'll need to extract those from the Spanish list.\n\nLet's create a mapping (cues number -> text). Already have text.\n\nWe need to also capture timestamps for each phrase (start time, just the start). Good.\n\nNow need usage note: short one-sentence usage note.\n\nWe also need translation for each phrase (into English). Since we have English subtitles, we can find the aligned English line via constant offset of +4 seconds. So we need to find the English cue whose start time is roughly phrase start + 4 seconds. We'll calculate.\n\nWe'll map each Spanish start to English start = Spanish start + 4 seconds offset (maybe exact 4 seconds). Because Spanish start 00:00:04 corresponds to English start 00:00:08. Indeed +4. Check others: Spanish 00:00:07.640 => English 00:00:11.640 (+4). So the offset is exactly +4 seconds.\n\nThus translation line: locate English cue with start time equal to Spanish start + 4 seconds (rounded to milliseconds). Use the English text.\n\nNow we need to find English lines.\n\nEnglish file provided earlier (list of cues). Let's map English cues:\n\nFirst few:\n\n1 00:00:08,000 - Good morning! What can I get you?\n\n2 00:00:11,640 - A coffee with milk, please.\n\n3 00:00:15,585 - For here or to go?\n\n4 00:00:19,890 - To go. I'm a bit in a hurry.\n\n5 00:00:23,970 - Sure. Anything else?\n\n6 00:00:27,255 - No thanks. How much is it?\n\n7 00:00:31,175 - It's two euros fifty.\n\n8 00:00:34,815 - Can I pay by card?\n\n9 00:00:38,540 - Yes, no problem.\n\n10 00:00:42,075 - Lena! What are you doing here?\n\n11 00:00:45,660 - Marco! What a coincidence.\n\n12 00:00:49,275 - Have you moved in yet?\n\n13 00:00:52,865 - Not yet. I'm still looking for a flat.\n\n14 00:00:56,670 - Really? And how's the search going?\n\n15 00:01:00,890 - So-so. Everything is really expensive.\n\n16 00:01:04,920 - I completely understand.\n\n17 00:01:08,450 - I have a visit at eleven today.\n\n18 00:01:12,560 - Do you want me to accompany you?\n\n19 00:01:16,535 - No need, but thanks.\n\n20 00:01:20,175 - Hey, it's going to rain. Do you have an umbrella?\n\n21 00:01:24,450 - No, I left it at home.\n\n22 00:01:28,425 - Here, take mine.\n\n23 00:01:31,680 - Are you sure?\n\n24 00:01:34,800 - Absolutely sure. Return it tonight.\n\n25 00:01:39,545 - OK. I owe you one.\n\n26 00:01:42,635 - Don't worry. Shall we have dinner together at nine?\n\n27 00:01:47,460 - Sounds great.\n\n28 00:01:50,995 - Perfec